<title>LUXEMBOURG: Court set to rule on Working Hours directive on 12 November.</title>
<headline>Court set to rule on Working Hours directive on 12 November.</headline>
<dateline>LUXEMBOURG 1996-10-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>The European Court of Justice said on Friday it will rule on November 12 in the controversial working hours directive case brought by Britain against the European Commission (C-84/94).</p>
<p>Britain has asked the court to annul the working hours directive, 104/93/EC, arguing it was improperly adopted as a health and safety measure with a qualified majority vote instead of under procedures requiring unanimity.</p>
<p>Advocate General Phillipe Leger advised the court last March that Britain's argument was unfounded, saying &quot;health and safety&quot; issues should be interpreted broadly.</p>
<p>Under the directive, employers cannot require employees to work more than 48 hours a week.</p>
</text>
